{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q016_pdb_1acc_domain_split","answer":"In SCOP 1.75, PDB 1acc chain A (anthrax protective antigen, UniProt P13423, 735 residues) is a single whole-chain domain, d1acca_ (SCCS f.11.1.1, 'Anthrax protective antigen', class f, membrane and cell surface proteins). The 1.75 record gives only the chain-wide description '1acc A:' and no residue ranges. In the current release I checked (SCOPe 2.08), the same chain is split into two domains: d1acca2 at residues 14-225 (b.179.1.1, PA14 family, fold b.179 'Anthrax protective antigen N-terminal-like', class b, all beta proteins) and d1acca1 at residues 226-735 (f.60.1.1, 'Anthrax protective antigen C-terminal-like', fold f.60, class f). So the change is a re-partition of one unit into two modules with different lineages. The 1.75 fold f.11 is no longer assigned to either module, and the N-terminal module moved to a different class. I did not check intermediate releases, so I describe only the 1.75 versus 2.08 difference.

What it reveals: a domain in SCOPe is a classification unit (a structural/evolutionary module), not a fixed property of the chain. The old single-domain call treated the whole protein as one evolutionary unit. The 2.08 split recognizes that the N-terminal region is a PA14-like beta module, and the C-terminal region is a separate alpha/beta-type module with its own superfamily. Each module's homology is judged on its own. The sequences are unchanged; the re-assignment reflects structural and evolutionary interpretation. The 2.08 representative 3tewA shares about 97% sequence identity with 1accA, which shows that the change is classification, not new sequence data.

Homology-search implications: (1) Do not treat 1acc A, or any whole-chain hit, as a single domain. Search or compare the N-terminal (14-225) and C-terminal (226-735) regions separately, and assign each hit to its module. (2) A hit to the old 1.75 whole-chain domain, or to f.11.1.1, should not be carried over as a homology claim for the full chain. Re-check it against the 2.08 modules. (3) A match that covers only one module supports only that module's superfamily or family, not the other. The PA14 module (b.179.1.1) and the f.60.1.1 module share no superfamily in these records, so a fold or class match to one should not be extended to the other. (4) The lineage here is the classification of the reference domains; it is not a transfer of classification to a matched target, and it does not establish domain count, exact boundaries, or function for other proteins. Coordinates or structure-based hits should be re-checked on the 2.08 residue ranges before any conclusion about homology.","evidence_used":["mcp__scope__get_scop_service_info (current release 2.08)","mcp__scope__get_scop_chain 1accA release 2.08: /api/v1/releases/2.08/chains/1accA","mcp__scope__get_scop_chain 1accA release 1.75: /api/v1/releases/1.75/chains/1accA","mcp__scope__get_scop_pdb_entry 1acc release 2.08 domains: /api/v1/releases/2.08/pdb/1acc/domains","mcp__scope__get_scop_domains d1acca1, d1acca2 parents release 2.08: /api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1acca1/parents, /api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1acca2/parents","mcp__scope__get_scop_domains d1acca_ parents release 1.75: /api/v1/releases/1.75/sids/d1acca_/parents"]}]}
